        "teacherMoves": "Display the photograph. Say: 'Look carefully at this farm in Laikipia County. A centre-pivot system is a long pipe that rotates around a central water source. What do you notice?' Allow 10 seconds of silent observation (WAIT TIME). Then: 'Write your own answers to the two questions on your sticky note. You have 2 minutes, do not confer yet.' Circulate silently, noting the range of predictions. After the 1-minute partner share, cold-call 3 learners: one who said A = πr² is sufficient, one who said it is not, and one who named a shape other than a ring. Record key phrases verbatim under 'Our Predictions'. Do NOT correct errors yet. Tell learners: 'In this unit each lesson looks at a different pivot on the same farm, with its own pipe lengths, so the numbers will change from lesson to lesson while the farm stays the same.' (Teacher decision on journal item math_2_6-076: keep the different sizes used in the lessons.) Say: 'By the end of today you will know which of these ideas are right.'",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Predict-before-instruction (anticipatory brainstorm). By committing predictions to paper before any instruction, learners activate prior knowledge of the circle area formula, surface misconceptions (e.g. 'just subtract the radii'), and generate genuine curiosity about why the full-circle formula is insufficient. Recording predictions publicly creates accountability and a reference point for revision.",
